+/*
+ * The skip memo (VitaSurf). The parser asks js_parse_skip_parens_token
+ * at a bracket what follows its match -- whether '(' starts an arrow
+ * function, whether '[' or '{' is a destructuring pattern -- and it
+ * answers by tokenizing everything up to the match. Code n brackets deep
+ * was tokenized n + 1 times, and a plugin bundle is one call wrapped
+ * around all of its code: 36% of the compile of Home Assistant's cards
+ * was this lookahead. The scan now notes the answer for every bracket
+ * it closes on the way, and the parser's own question at that bracket
+ * later is a look-up.
+ *
+ * A bracket's answer is kept only if nothing in it can tokenize
+ * differently where the parser meets it: whether '/' starts a regular
+ * expression depends on the token before it, and await, yield and the
+ * strict mode reserved words are keywords in some functions and names
+ * in others. A '/' after one of those leaves the enclosing brackets
+ * unkept, to be scanned in their own place as before. Short brackets
+ * are not kept either: scanning them again costs less than the table.
+ */
+#define SKIP_MEMO_MIN_SPAN 48
